Windows Security Investigation
Overview
In this project, I conducted a hands-on Windows security investigation to gain practical experience with authentication monitoring, endpoint visibility, and event log analysis. Using Windows Event Viewer, PowerShell, and Microsoft Sysmon, I collected and analyzed security events to understand how Windows records user activity and system processes.

Environment
Operating System: Windows 11 Pro
Hostname: Hamse-Barood-HP
Tools Used:
Event Viewer
Windows Security Auditing
PowerShell
Microsoft Sysmon
Findings
Successful Logon Activity (Event ID 4624)
I reviewed Windows Security logs and identified multiple successful logon events. The selected event showed the SYSTEM account successfully authenticating on the local machine.

Assessment: Expected activity. SYSTEM account logons are generated by legitimate Windows services and background processes.

Recommendation: Establish a baseline of normal authentication activity and regularly review logon events for anomalies.

Failed Logon Activity (Event ID 4625)
I generated and captured a controlled failed logon attempt to verify that audit logging was functioning correctly.

Assessment: Expected activity. The event was intentionally created for testing and does not indicate malicious behavior.

Recommendation: Monitor for repeated failed logon attempts and configure alerts to detect potential brute-force or password-spraying attacks.

Process Creation Monitoring (Sysmon Event ID 1)
After deploying Sysmon, I verified that process creation events were being recorded successfully. Sysmon captured the launch of Event Viewer (mmc.exe) and its parent process (explorer.exe).

Assessment: Expected administrative activity. The process execution was directly related to the investigation workflow.

Recommendation: Maintain Sysmon monitoring and implement an enhanced configuration to improve endpoint visibility and threat detection.

PowerShell-Based Log Analysis
I used PowerShell to retrieve and analyze security events from both Windows Security and Sysmon logs.

Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Security'; Id=4624,4625} -MaxEvents 20
Get-WinEvent -FilterHashtable @{LogName='Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational'; Id=1} -MaxEvents 20
Assessment: The queries successfully returned authentication and process-creation events, confirming that auditing and monitoring were working as expected.

Recommendation: Incorporate PowerShell-based log collection into regular security monitoring and incident response workflows.

